lore.content:
Reuse first, split on doubt. A name in new material is taken to be an existing person when any of these holds:
the owner plainly treats it as that person ("found them in 1881!", "this is Joseph's will");
it appears in the same name form, role and setting as an existing record (the same household in the next census; the same family in the same parish register);
the material is an index, copy or derivative of a source already attached to that person, or the original of one: an index entry and its register entry are one entry, so one person.
Then attach the new claims to the existing person, add the new name form to aliases (read, then write the full list), and say so in the reply: "I've taken the Joe Callen in this deed to be your Joseph; tell me if that's wrong." Never silently.
Keep a separate, record-qualified person ("Joe Callen (1902 deed)") with a same-person claim only when there is a real reason to doubt: a place, period, age or role that does not fit; a common name with nothing linking it; evidence against; or the owner's doubt ("is this our Joseph?"). New dates or details for a link already recorded update that claim, whichever name form the new record uses. Two existing records are joined only on the owner's word. A research question that is itself about identity ("whether he's the Elias who went to sea") is not doubt about each record; weigh each record on its own fit. The first time in a new session that you rely on a name you took to be an existing person, say so again.
